59 — THE “BILLION-DOLLAR” TEST

Before anything ships, score:

Dimension	Question
Identity	Does this unmistakably feel like NayaNET?
Clarity	Does the user immediately know what to do?
Beauty	Would this look at home in a world-class product?
Depth	Does it feel physical rather than flat?
Interaction	Does touching it feel extraordinary?
Intelligence	Does Naya actually make the experience smarter?
Context	Does it respond to what matters now?
Emotion	Does the user feel something?
Simplicity	Can anything be removed?
Utility	Does the interaction produce real value?
Performance	Does it feel instantaneous?
Accessibility	Can everyone use it?
Consistency	Does it belong to the same world?

If any major category is below 9/10, it isn't finished.

60 — THE SCREENSHOT TEST IS DEAD

A screenshot can be beautiful and the interface can still be terrible.

Therefore:

NEVER APPROVE BASED ON SCREENSHOTS ALONE.

Test:

hover → focus → touch → press → loading → active → success → back → return → mobile → keyboard → reduced motion

The Living Interface standard explicitly makes interaction states and touch behavior part of the quality bar rather than treating visual appearance as sufficient.

